decisions:
  - id: TD-2026-08-11-lens-ignore
    decision: Code lenses delegate file enumeration to a shared walk that applies BOTH a never-meaning baseline AND the repository's own root .warplineignore/.gitignore, reusing the same matcher the worktree snapshot path uses.
    rationale: Per-lens hardcoded denylists diverged from each other and from the snapshot path, and none encoded project intent. Sharing the snapshot's matcher makes 'what a lens lifts' and 'what a snapshot seals' agree by construction rather than by vigilance. Nested ignore files stay out of scope at v1, so .build remains in the baseline.
  - id: TD-2026-08-11-both-writers
    decision: health.contested sums shadow-observed AND ledger-recorded contested verdicts, reported split, counted through the same reader `warpline knot` uses.
    rationale: The two populations are disjoint by construction (a --shadow run deliberately persists no payload), so summing cannot double-count. Reading only one writer made the instrument report zero for the entire native era. Sharing the reader prevents the two surfaces from drifting apart about what exists.
learnings:
  - THE DRESS REHEARSAL WAS THE CHEAPEST TEST AVAILABLE AND IT WAS DEFERRED LONGEST. Everything was component-verified and nothing was lifecycle-verified; one scripted end-to-end run on a fresh repo found three defects in under an hour, including the one that had been distorting the programme's headline belief. Component tests pass on a system whose parts cannot be reached in sequence.
  - 'AN INSTRUMENT THAT READS ONE OF TWO WRITERS REPORTS THE FAVOURABLE ANSWER ABOUT ITS OWN EMPTINESS. `health` counted contested verdicts from shadow rows alone while the native seal path persists payloads to .warpline/knots/ and writes no shadow row. A real KNOT sat on disk one directory over while the diagnostic printed ''ZERO contested verdicts''. This is the merge-tree exit-1 conflation lesson recurring in a second location — the same failure SHAPE, so the audit question generalizes: for every counter, enumerate ALL writers before trusting a zero.'
  - A ZERO FROM AN INSTRUMENT IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE INSTRUMENT UNTIL PROVEN OTHERWISE. The belief that this project had never produced a KNOT was partly a measurement artifact. Before treating an empty denominator as a fact about the world, drive the system to produce ONE of the thing by hand and check the instrument sees it.
  - INHERITED NUMBERS DECAY — RE-MEASURE BEFORE ACTING ON THEM. The checkpoint's '13,179 of 16,035 born, .build/** or node_modules' was wrong in magnitude (14,168 of 25,341), wrong in mechanism (cfg-lens key-lifting, not the snapshot path), and wrong in attribution (node_modules contributes exactly zero). Acting on it directly would have produced a fix aimed at the wrong walker.
  - NAME THE SURFACE A NUMBER BELONGS TO, OR THE NUMBER WILL BE READ AS GLOBAL. The 55.9%-junk figure was true of `absorb WORKTREE` and FALSE of the sealed ledger, because seals ride `git archive` (git-tracked files only) while the worktree walk sees everything on disk. Stated without that qualifier it produced a false alarm about a 16,015-symbol mass retirement; the real seal delta was +2. Warpline has THREE absorb surfaces (git-ref, worktree, native snapshot) with different visibility — every measurement must say which one it came from.
  - 'HARDCODED DENYLISTS ENCODE THE ECOSYSTEMS SOMEONE HAPPENED TO THINK OF; .gitignore ENCODES THE PROJECT. Two lenses carried private skip-lists that had silently diverged, and neither read the repo''s own ignore rules — so Swift''s .build was invisible to both. Measured on an Expo-shaped fixture: 3,673 symbols without ignore-awareness vs 13 with it, and the junk sources (ios/Pods, .expo) are reachable ONLY through .gitignore — no baseline denylist would have caught them.'
  - 'SYMBOL IDS THAT EMBED ABSOLUTE PATHS SILENTLY DESTROY THE CORE PROPERTY. #cfg ids carried /Users/ascend/... , making the state''s content-address machine-dependent — the exact thing the WARP exists to guarantee. Worth an explicit invariant test, because nothing else would have caught it.'
  - A FLAKY RED TRAINS THE READER TO DISCOUNT THE SIGNAL. Concurrent `git worktree add` calls all named their leaf `tree`, colliding in git's check-then-create window on `.git/worktrees/<basename>`; it surfaced as three DIFFERENT unrelated tests failing on three different runs. Diagnosing it required removing my own new tests and re-running to see the suite fail WORSE without them — exonerate-or-convict by experiment, never by plausibility.
